**Idea:** The baseline computes T4 and T6 with extra ciphertext multiplies, but the output only needs the odd terms T1, T3, T5, T7. The odd Chebyshev polynomials can be built with four ciphertext multiplies: T2 = 2z²−1, T3 = 2zT2−z, T5 = 2T2·T3−z, T7 = 2T2·T5−T3. That replaces six ct-ct multiplies with four and reduces the depth from 8 to 6. The dropped even coefficients (c2, c4, c6) are ~1e-16, so they don't affect correctness. The final sum is c0 + c1·z + c3·T3 + c5·T5 + c7·T7, using plaintext scalars.

```cpp
// Chebyshev hardshrink degree 7 on [-4,4].
// Only odd Chebyshev terms are needed (even coefficients ~1e-16).
// T2 = 2z^2-1, T3 = 2z*T2 - z, T5 = 2*T2*T3 - z, T7 = 2*T2*T5 - T3.
// Four ciphertext-ciphertext multiplies instead of six, depth 6.

void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c3("c3");
  Plaintext c5("c5");
  Plaintext c7("c7");

  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;

  Ciphertext zz = z * z;
  Ciphertext T2 = zz + zz - one;

  Ciphertext zT2 = z * T2;
  Ciphertext T3 = zT2 + zT2 - z;

  Ciphertext T2T3 = T2 * T3;
  Ciphertext T5 = T2T3 + T2T3 - z;

  Ciphertext T2T5 = T2 * T5;
  Ciphertext T7 = T2T5 + T2T5 - T3;

  Ciphertext t1 = z * c1;
  Ciphertext t3 = T3 * c3;
  Ciphertext t5 = T5 * c5;
  Ciphertext t7 = T7 * c7;

  Ciphertext result = t1 + t3;
  result = result + t5;
  result = result + t7;
  result = result + c0;

  result.set_output("y");
}
```

**Idea:** The kernel already uses the minimum I can see for this structure: four ciphertext-ciphertext multiplies (T2, T3, T5, T7) and one ciphertext-plaintext multiply per coefficient. I tried alternative groupings. Paterson-Stockmeyer-style evaluation of y = z·Q(T2) needs at least five ct-ct multiplies, so it is worse. Pre-multiplying c7 into T2 adds ct-pt multiplies and does not remove a ct-ct one. The only change left is the summation order. I add the deeper terms first so that the mismatched-level alignments happen on fewer and cheaper operands. I expect only a small gain, so this is a low-risk refinement of attempt 1, not a large speedup.

